1
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,242
Welcome to Noema, our banking assistant prototype for the Factored Data Hackathon.

2
00:00:12,242 --> 00:00:17,983
This walkthrough follows a customer from account questions to a credit inquiry and a request for human support.

3
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,552
Step one: establish the customer context.

4
00:00:27,552 --> 00:00:31,785
Sandra introduces herself, and the interface loads her demo profile.

5
00:00:31,785 --> 00:00:37,698
This is a demonstration identity flow; it does not establish production banking authentication.

6
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,437
On the right, the step-by-step panel displays rules, observations, and evidence.

7
00:00:49,437 --> 00:00:56,481
It distinguishes achieved checks from missing and pending items, giving reviewers a view of the system behind the conversation.

8
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,421
Step two: ask about the customer profile.

9
00:01:11,421 --> 00:01:13,546
Sandra requests a financial summary.

10
00:01:13,546 --> 00:01:20,632
This broad request is not resolved in the recording, so she follows up by naming the specific account details she wants.

11
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,291
The assistant returns a recorded balance.

12
00:01:37,291 --> 00:01:43,269
Notice that the request also included income and active products, but the response covers only the balance.

13
00:01:43,269 --> 00:01:46,510
This is a partial answer, and a useful improvement target.

14
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,527
Step three: ask focused account questions.

15
00:01:57,527 --> 00:02:02,220
The balance is repeated consistently, followed by an estimated monthly income.

16
00:02:02,220 --> 00:02:07,514
For a clearer banking experience, the income response should also identify its currency.

17
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,311
Step four: explore credit eligibility.

18
00:02:20,311 --> 00:02:22,197
Sandra asks about credit cards.

19
00:02:22,197 --> 00:02:25,602
Noema requests an amount and currency before continuing.

20
00:02:25,602 --> 00:02:30,164
The intended design separates the conversation from the eligibility policy.

21
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,134
Sandra enters a ten-thousand-dollar credit card request.

22
00:02:47,134 --> 00:02:49,988
As the response arrives, review the proposed terms.

23
00:02:49,988 --> 00:02:55,864
The recording alone does not prove that those terms match a completed, customer-specific policy decision.

24
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:05,659
The answer switches to Spanish.

25
00:03:05,659 --> 00:03:07,531
Sandra asks to continue in English.

26
00:03:07,531 --> 00:03:13,309
This shows why preserving the preferred language throughout the workflow matters to the customer experience.

27
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:30,378
Step five: review the response and request the next action.

28
00:03:30,378 --> 00:03:35,589
The assistant describes a credit limit and rate, then asks whether Sandra wants to proceed.

29
00:03:35,589 --> 00:03:39,826
An offer in chat is not confirmation that an application has been created.

30
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:50,831
Step six: request human support.

31
00:03:50,831 --> 00:03:53,749
Noema says it cannot proceed and offers an advisor.

32
00:03:53,749 --> 00:04:00,214
The clip ends with a referral message; a completed handoff still needs a visible case reference and confirmation.
